Netplay host thread safety (Melee-NX-NetPlay 0.4.2): the game thread, the matchmaking thread and the netplay thread
shared objects without locks. Found by the console crash of 0.4.1 (newlib's malloc on a corrupted free list during a
search after failed connection attempts) and by ThreadSanitizer on Linux (frontend/test-online-linux.sh with
MU_SANITIZE=thread MU_DEAD_FIRST=N). Applied by ../net_sources.sh after net-0001; port/ is unchanged.
- Matchmaking: the players, the match result, the stages and the error text are written by the matchmaking thread
  at every match found and copied by the game thread every frame (0xB3: GetPlayerName, GetPlayerInfo, ...): now
  built aside and published under a lock; the local player index and the service session id are atomics; a second
  FindMatch on a thread still joinable no longer ends the program.
- User: user.json is read again by the game thread (0xB9) while the matchmaking thread copies the record: a lock.
- NetplayClient: the time sync (frame offsets, last frame timings, game started), the acknowledged frames and their
  timers, the remote selections and the remote synced states were shared by the game and netplay threads without
  locks (a vector copied while the other thread appended to it); the peer health time was a static shared by clients.
- net_diag: the netplay thread wrote sample rows to the file the game thread closes at a cleanup; a lock, and the
  retrace and ping are taken on the game thread. The online frame counter is an atomic.
- The matchmaking port generator is shared by the old and the new Matchmaking objects after a cleanup: a lock.
